{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1742 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "0e434c18d83b77ab51c9062bd8a22be899cf35f72c751a4e4e256088f847eef2" :edb/db-before-t 1741 :edb/db-after-t 1742 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535054 50 #inst "2026-10-08T15:45:18.331Z" 13194139535054 true] [17592186151933 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14050897/assets/7325a35720157e2331ade1b9abd042f90030361c1560dd3bf98de08cc495e4c4.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14050897/assets/d1457393a0b919a208da95e0b819b50a83847d2a5dfbb9c4c81c4c457a2cd467.png)\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\n- $R=[-2,2] \\times [-2,1]$\n\n- $R=[-2,1] \\times [-2,2]$\n\n- $R=[-1,1] \\times [-1,1]$" 13194139535054 true] [17592186151933 1100 "Consider the region $D$ shown above. If\n${x^{2} - y^{2},\\begin{vmatrix}(x, y) ∈ R∩D \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}(x, y) ∈ R∖D$\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2616/Source/Images/f62ea4c35fcc7d18eac85d6d253ef727.png)\n\n1. $R=[-2,2] \\times [-2,1]$\n2. $R=[-2,1] \\times [-2,2]$\n3. $R=[-1,1] \\times [-1,1]$" 13194139535054 false] [17592186151933 1145 "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define\n\n$$\n{x^{2} - y^{2},\\begin{vmatrix}(x, y) ∈ R∩D \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}(x, y) ∈ R∖D\n$$\n\nthen, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D (x^2-y^2) \\: \\text{d}A = \\iint\\limits_R f(x,y) \\: \\text{d}A\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangles I and II cover the region $D$ completely.\n- Rectangle III does not cover the region $D$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139535054 false] [17592186151933 1145 "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14050897/assets/8e0d0d508d61352edf461f147cc561a527f6b3191e6c6b11d070eedf1e6572e3.png)\n\nthen, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D (x^2-y^2) \\: \\text{d}A = \\iint\\limits_R f(x,y) \\: \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangles I and II cover the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Rectangle III does not cover the region $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139535054 true] [17592186152124 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328744 13194139535054 true] [17592186152124 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328751 13194139535054 true] [17592186152745 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535054 true] [17592186152745 1100 "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curve $x^{2} + \\frac{y^{2}}{4} = 1$. If\n$f(x, y) = {xy,\\begin{vmatrix}(x, y) ∈ R∩D \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}(x, y) ∈ R∖Dand∬_(D)xydA = ∬_(R)f(x, y)dA$,\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R$?\n\n1. $R = [- 2, 2] \\cdot [- 1, 1]$\n2. $R = [- 1, 1] \\cdot [- 2, 2]$\n3. $R = [- 2, 2] \\cdot [- 2, 2]$" 13194139535054 false] [17592186152745 1100 "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curve ${x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1.$ If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}xy, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\n- $R=[-2,2]\\times [-1,1]$\n- $R=[-1,1]\\times [-2,2]$\n- $R=[-2,2]\\times [-2,2]$" 13194139535054 true] [17592186152745 1145 "First, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14050897/assets/736575dceb6969f8b45e4038f702ebc84347ab66a1a8e5ea5103840ddd61ba31.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}xy, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangle I does not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Rectangles II and III cover the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535054 true] [17592186328744 1017 #edb/ref 17592186328745 13194139535054 true] [17592186328744 1089 "q-86860" 13194139535054 true] [17592186328744 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535054 true] [17592186328744 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14050897/assets/4772b690cb85fcb4dffd27ac4ba56344307ca106e927b9945144370b266d2be9.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\n- $R=[0,2]\\times [-1,1]$\n- $R=[-1,0]\\times [0,1]$\n- $R=[-1,2]\\times [-2,2]$" 13194139535054 true] [17592186328744 1113 #uuid "7f020700-6aa9-5008-bb57-88129d05abec" 13194139535054 true] [17592186328744 1145 "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangles I and II do not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Rectangle III covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139535054 true] [17592186328745 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328746 13194139535054 true] [17592186328745 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328747 13194139535054 true] [17592186328745 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328748 13194139535054 true] [17592186328745 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328749 13194139535054 true] [17592186328745 100